        "id": 18338
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-06-09T00:10:32Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Actually, thinking about this some more, expires_at would get set at password create/update. For existing deployments though, if they turn this on, when would their current passwords expire? Not until the user updates their password, which is not what I would expect. I would expect all passwords at that point to expire after 90 days.",
      "parentUuid": "7aa08908_b0b84912",
      "revId": "1fd49dbc8620177b0e6798d035319a0163da070b",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7aa08908_2b850ace",
        "filename": "keystone/common/sql/migrate_repo/versions/103_add_new_password_columns.py",
        "patchSetId": 52
      },
      "lineNbr": 27,
      "author": {
        "id": 4
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-06-09T00:46:12Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e we\u0027re possibly authenticating against multiple passwords\n\nYou could avoid that by setting currently-future expires_at to *now* when you do a change password operation, so that they\u0027re immediately expired.\n\nAs for upgrades, it\u0027d be pretty trivial to document a SQL command in the release notes to introduce expiring passwords (update password set expires_at\u003d(+90 days) where expires_at is null)",
      "parentUuid": "7aa08908_d0a99525",
      "revId": "1fd49dbc8620177b0e6798d035319a0163da070b",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "7aa08908_dfece494",
        "filename": "keystone/common/sql/migrate_repo/versions/103_add_new_password_columns.py",
        "patchSetId": 52
      },
      "lineNbr": 27,
      "author": {
        "id": 18338
      },
      "writtenOn": "2016-06-09T13:52:48Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Yeah, but if the expectation is that all passwords should expire in 90 days when I change the config setting, why not just implement that functionality?  \n\nLikewise, if I have a user in the whitelist, where their password doesn\u0027t expire, and then I remove them from the whitelist, operators will also have to run a SQL command.\n\nWhile this may not seem like a big deal, I\u0027m not sure what we are getting out of it, having passwords that have different expiration times. What is the use case for this? I mean, I\u0027ve never been able to log into a system with 2 different passwords.",
      "parentUuid": "7aa08908_2b850ace",
      "revId": "1fd49dbc8620177b0e6798d035319a0163da070b",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
